   "html_text": "Most paradoxes considered so far involve negation in an essential way, e.g. sentences saying of themselves that they are not true or knowable. The central role of negation will become even clearer when we formalise the paradoxes of self-reference in Section 2 below. Curry’s paradox is a similar paradox of self-reference that however does not directly involve negation. A semantic variant of Curry’s paradox comes from the following Curry sentence ⟦M⟧ : “If this sentence is true then ⟦M⟧ ”, where ⟦",
